hi I'm Nick Gillespie with Reason TV I'm  joined by Matt Welch my colleague at  Reason magazine and today we're talking  with John Mackey the co-founder and CEO  of Whole Foods Market John thanks for  coming thanks for having me you in  August penned an op-ed in the Wall  Street Journal outlining the Whole Foods  medical plan and this caused an enormous  stir and a season where we're talking  about healthcare reform why do we need  health care reform and why should the  government not be as big a part of Gov  health care the current system the way  it's structured and regulated is  becoming increasingly more and more  expensive and we've gone from spending  four percent of our gross domestic  product on health care in 1960 to almost  17 percent today and the trend lines  aren't really slowing down so it's  becoming increasingly expensive for the  whole society and so that I think that's  what's driving the need for health care  reform so then why why should the  government get out of health care  because government spends about half of  every health care I don't think I  suggested that government get out of  health care but that rather than as you  say they spend almost 50% of all the  health care dollars the other 50% they  don't spend they highly regulate I think  that we need to empower individuals in  such a way and empower markets I think  that's a better way to drive down the  cost of health care for all of us do you  think employer-based health care is a  right or is even a good idea I'm not  sure that's the best way to do it  primarily because as long as you work  for Whole Foods we got this great health  insurance program but what if you want  to leave what if you get a better job  offer someplace else oh you're ready to  do something else you can't take it's  not portable you can't take Whole Foods  plan with you so that restrains people  from maybe leaving they're not sure they  can get as good a health care program  and so I don't think it's the best way  we could do it make me better if  individuals did it themselves  can you talk a little bit about your  sort of politics how you would describe  them how they originated just  political philosophy I believe in  capitalism I believe in markets I  believe in an individual's individual  empowerment an individual individual  choice so that's my philosophy of  politics and capitalism one the 20th  century I mean the battle between  communism and capitalism as a basic  economic system  are we still winning or are we  floundering in in the wake of that big  Viktor again I think as I think we won  but we didn't people didn't come to love  capitalism because I don't think we've  articulated well we haven't made people  conscious of what capitalism is and all  the good things that it does it's always  on the defensive it's always Malhar  always attacking it for being greedy and  selfish and only caring about money all  you care about is money we've created  this wall in the world between on the  one hand we believe that not-for-profits  and government are motivated by a deeper  purpose public service and then on the  other hand side of this wall we have  corporations and businesses and they're  motivated strictly by selfishness and  greed and frankly most young idealistic  people are more drawn towards the one  that's not motivated strictly by  selfishness do you have any political  team that you particularly root for a  political team it's funny that you would  say that I think part of that  polarization that exists in America is  that people try to size you up for what  team you're on if you're on their team  they love you and if you're on the other  team they hate you I believe people need  to think for themselves and so I don't  feel I come on any of those teams did  you vote in the 2008 election I did yeah  who did you vote for I voted for a Bob  Barr Libertarian candidate I was a you  know electron Paul but he didn't get the  Republican nomination regretfully right  well thank you John I want to thank John  Mackey of Whole Foods for talking with  reason TV I'm Nick Gillespie with Matt  Welch
 

